<h2 id="start-in-albuquerque">How to start a side hustle in Albuquerque this month</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Pick one main hustle and one seasonal add-on.</strong> For example, delivery all year plus Fiesta work in October.</li>
  <li><strong>Register the right way.</strong> Get a New Mexico tax ID for gross receipts tax through the Taxation and Revenue Department's online portal, and file your $35 city business registration.</li>
  <li><strong>Build GRT into your prices.</strong> Quote prices that cover the roughly 7.6% you will owe, or state it on invoices.</li>
  <li><strong>Get in front of locals.</strong> Nextdoor, neighborhood Facebook groups and a free Google Business Profile work well for home services.</li>
  <li><strong>Collect three reviews fast.</strong> Ask early clients to review you, and offer referral discounts.</li>
  <li><strong>Track income and expenses separately.</strong> A dedicated account makes GRT and income tax filings much easier.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="new-mexico-taxes">New Mexico taxes and rules for side hustles</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> graduated rates from 1.5% to 5.9%.</li>
  <li><strong>Gross receipts tax (GRT):</strong> New Mexico taxes most services as well as goods, which is unusual. The tax is imposed on you as the seller. The state rate is 4.875%, and local add-ons bring the combined rate in most of Albuquerque to about 7.6%. Rates change, so check the Taxation and Revenue Department's current rate schedule.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax:</strong> 15.3% federal on net profit, plus federal income tax. Estimate it with the <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>City registration:</strong> businesses inside Albuquerque city limits register with the city for $35 a year. Rio Rancho, Bernalillo County and other jurisdictions have their own rules.</li>
  <li><strong>Handyman limits:</strong> a state handyman certificate allows minor work up to $7,200 a year, excluding electrical, plumbing and gas.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><strong>Quick tip:</strong> GRT you collect is not your money. Move it into a separate savings account each week so it is ready when your return is due.</div>

